rlm-gar-version-rm [-n|--dry-run] [-r|--refresh]

Delete individual versions inside a single Artifact Registry package.
Two pickers: first choose one <repo>/<package>, then TAB-multi-select
the versions (digests) inside it to delete.

Where gar-rm removes a whole package, this prunes selected versions
and leaves the package in place — the tool for dropping old builds
while keeping the current ones.

The package picker is the shared gar-* picker: same cache, same MRU
history, same --- REFRESH CACHE --- partial-refresh flow as gar-open
and gar-rm.

The version picker lists every version oldest-first — the old builds
are what a delete tool is for — showing date, size, short digest,
and tags (untagged versions are dimmed). Versions whose date the API
does not report sort first, ahead of the dated ones. The
preview pane shows the image name, every tag on the digest, the full
SHA, size, media type (flagging multi-arch indexes), upload/update/
create/build times, platform, and any other scalar metadata the API
returns. A digest carrying more than one tag is called out
explicitly, since deleting it removes all of them.

MULTI-ARCH IMAGES

The per-architecture images behind a multi-arch tag appear in the
registry listing as ordinary untagged versions, with nothing tying
them to the index that references them — so a flat list makes them
indistinguishable from genuine leftovers. They are not: deleting one
breaks the tagged image for that platform, and the tag keeps
resolving, so the result is pull failures rather than a clean
absence.

Each index is therefore resolved to its members, and the picker shows

  2026-08-11    ?   sha256:f79f208bf156…  pr-3765 (index, 4 arch)
  2026-08-11   58MB sha256:007830616219…  └─ linux/amd64 of pr-3765
  2026-08-11   59MB sha256:5a19e04d38ec…  └─ linux/arm64 of pr-3765
  2026-08-11   33KB sha256:5be1c2a52f50…  └─ attestation of pr-3765

with members grouped directly under their index and each family kept
contiguous and sorted by the age of the index, not of the member.
Buildx attestation/provenance manifests report no platform and are
labelled "attestation" — they are members too, not orphans. An index
that carries no tag of its own is flagged "(untagged index, N arch)"
rather than a bare "(untagged)", because deleting it strands the
versions beneath it. The preview names the relationship in both
directions: a member says what it BELONGS TO, an index lists the
versions it references.

Only a version that is neither tagged nor referenced by any index is
shown as a plain "(untagged)".

CACHING

The listing and the index attribution are cached per package under
~/.cache/gar-version/<hash>/. The listing carries a 10-minute TTL;
the attribution is kept indefinitely, because a digest names an
immutable manifest — what an index points at cannot change without
the index itself getting a different digest. Only indexes pushed
since the last run are fetched, so the per-run cost tracks new
builds rather than package size. A cold 205-version package with 41
indexes takes ~3s; reopening it takes ~0.2s. Moving through the
picker costs no API calls at all.

If children.tsv is truncated or pruned out of step with the record of
which indexes have been resolved, the mismatch is detected and the
affected indexes are re-fetched on the next run.

After a delete the listing is dropped and the removed digests are
purged from the attribution, so the next run cannot show versions
that no longer exist.

DELETION ORDER

The selection is reordered so that an index is always deleted before
the versions it references: the registry refuses to remove a manifest
while a live index still points at it. Picker order is nearly right
already, since families render parent-first, but it breaks for a
nested index — an index referencing another index — whose members can
sort into an earlier family than their grandparent. Sorting by depth
in the reference chain covers that, and also survives the 75-name
chunking, because any prefix of a parents-first list is itself
parents-first.

WHAT THE REGISTRY WILL REFUSE

Some selections cannot succeed no matter how they are ordered. These
are reported before the confirmation, with the reason, rather than
being discovered when a batch fails halfway through.

Members whose index is NOT part of the selection cannot be deleted at
all while that index remains. EVERY referencing index has to go, not
just one: a digest can be a member of several indexes at once (common
where successive builds share an identical layer set), and the rule
is that an artifact becomes deletable only once nothing points at it.
Each blocking index is named.

Repository-level conditions are reported too, since they apply
whatever was picked:

  - a repository with immutable tags refuses to delete any TAGGED
    version, because its tags cannot be modified, moved or deleted;
    untagged versions are unaffected, so the count of tagged versions
    in the selection is shown.

  - a REMOTE or VIRTUAL repository serves its artifacts from an
    upstream and cannot delete them at all.

Tagged versions are otherwise deletable: deleting by digest removes
the digest and its tags together. (The --delete-tags flag some gcloud
commands need applies to deleting by TAG, not to the batchDelete call
used here, which takes only names and validateOnly.)

DELETION

The exact API call is printed before a single confirmation
covering the whole selection. Deletion then runs via the Artifact
Registry REST endpoint:

  POST .../packages/<pkg>/versions:batchDelete
  { "names": [ ".../versions/sha256:..." , ... ] }

in chunks of at most 75 names, which is the server-enforced maximum
(76 is rejected). Each chunk's long-running operation is polled to
completion before the next is sent, so the verification below cannot
race the server.

There is no gcloud equivalent. `gcloud artifacts versions delete`
takes a SINGLE version positional and fails with "unrecognized
arguments: sha256:..." when handed a list; the same is true on the
alpha and beta tracks, `docker images delete` takes one image, and
gcloud exposes no generic REST caller. Deleting N versions through
gcloud would mean N invocations at ~0.8s of process startup each —
about 14 minutes for 1000 versions, against ~15s for batchDelete.

A clean return from batchDelete is NOT proof of deletion: the API
answers 200 and completes the operation without error for names that
do not exist. That is why the result is verified against a fresh
listing rather than trusted.

Afterwards the package is re-listed and each selected digest checked
against what remains. Any still present are reported and a single
retry offered. Selecting every version in a package deletes the
versions but leaves an empty package behind; that is called out
before you confirm, with a pointer to gar-rm.

The package picker then reopens, so several packages can be pruned in
one sitting. Aborting the PACKAGE picker (Ctrl-G, Esc) is what exits;
aborting the version picker or declining the confirmation just goes
back a step. --refresh applies only to the first package opened, so
later rounds still use the cache.

OPTIONS
  -n, --dry-run
              Show both pickers, the selection summary, and the exact
              commands, but run no deletes and ask no confirmation.

  -r, --refresh
              Ignore the cached listing and refetch it, regardless of
              the TTL. Already-resolved multi-arch indexes are still
              reused — they cannot go stale.

  -h, --help  Print usage and exit.

ENVIRONMENT
  GAR_SEARCH_PROJECTS
              Colon-separated list of GCP project IDs to browse.
              Required; shared with gar-open, gar-search and gar-rm.

  GAR_SEARCH_LOCATIONS
              Colon-separated Artifact Registry locations to scan
              (default europe:europe-west1). Repositories are
              listed per location — the API has no "all locations"
              query — so a location missing from this list is
              invisible to the picker.

REQUIRES
  gcloud, fzf, jq, curl, python3.

SEE ALSO
  gar-rm      Delete whole packages (all versions plus the package).
  gar-open    Browse packages and open one in the GCP console.
  gar-search  Non-interactive substring search over packages.

NOTE
  OSC 8 hyperlinks in the preview pane require a terminal emulator
  that supports them (Kitty, Ghostty, iTerm2, WezTerm). Inside tmux
  also set: set -ga terminal-features "*:hyperlinks"
